[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125111,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital naevus is a benign melanocytic naevus located in the genital region.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125112,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8720/0 Genital naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125113,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F20.Y &amp; XH5EL4 Other specific types of melanocytic naevus &amp; Genital naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125114,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> genital naevocytic naevus; naevus of the genital tract; naevus of special site.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125115,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125116,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In women, the labium minus is the most affected site, followed by the labium majus, clitoris, and vulvar vestibule; in men, the glans, shaft, and prepuce are most commonly affected {25830986; 9445124}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125117,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital naevi are uniformly pigmented, well-circumscribed, solitary macules or papules, up to 10&nbsp;mm in size, that typically arise in young adults.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125118,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital naevi account for &lt;&nbsp;5% of pigmented lesions in the genital area in women {25830986}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125119,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is similar to that of common naevi.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125120,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most genital naevi have <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutations {15161700; 24842760; 27220476}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125121,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:02:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125122,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>The junctional component consists of single cells and nests haphazardly distributed along the epidermal rete ridges, often oriented parallel to the epidermal surface, with focal retraction artefact and dyscohesion. The centre may have limited pagetoid scatter. The dermal component may show cytological atypia superficially, but shows maturation with increasing depth. Mitotic figures are very rare and only superficial {18162770}, with increased numbers in pregnancy. Lesions may be polypoid, have a dense lymphocytic infiltrate (halo phenomenon), or be associated with lichen sclerosus {11790170}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Mucosal melanoma is more common in older individuals than genital naevus, has more cytological atypia and mitoses, lacks maturation, shows patchy HMB45 immunoreactivity, and has a higher proliferation rate.</p>\r\n<p>Dysplastic naevi share many features with genital naevi, in particular the architectural disorder (bridging and lamellar fibrosis) {18976416}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:03:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
